  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14095797/assets/30b101fa9812c49237ab72c0c4aebd1666df6de80367eb2ee59b33fe0e422d87.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{\u00a0m}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{\u00a0m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-12\\text{\u00a0m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $12\\text{\u00a0m}/\\text{min}.$",
